# =============================================================================
# THE PRODUCTION HOST'S OWN ENVIRONMENT  (B7) — docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §٤-ج
# =============================================================================
# For the operations scripts that run ON THE SERVER, not in a container:
#   seed:catalog, seed:payment-methods, bootstrap:owner, db:prove-rls,
#   backup, backup:hourly, backup:fetch, restore-drill.
#
#   cp .env.host.example .env.host.local && chmod 600 .env.host.local
#   bash scripts/host-run.sh npm run <script>
#
# `.env.host.local` is ignored by git and by Docker (`.env.*.local`), and no
# compose file reads it: the superuser password below never reaches a
# container. scripts/host-run.sh refuses it unless it is mode 600.
#
# Every URL goes through B5 — 127.0.0.1:5432, published on loopback only by
# docker-compose.prod.yml. NOT `postgres:5432`: that name exists only inside
# the Docker network, which is why .env.production's URLs do not work here.
#
# BACKUP_* are NOT here: they stay in .env.production, and host-run.sh reads
# only those keys from it.
# =============================================================================

# Superuser — backup, backup:hourly, restore-drill, db:prove-rls.
#
# WITHOUT ITS PASSWORD, and host-run.sh refuses it otherwise. These scripts hand
# this URL to pg_dump, pg_dumpall, pg_restore and psql AS AN ARGUMENT, and every
# local user can read a process's arguments (`ps`, /proc/<pid>/cmdline). The
# password — POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD from .env.production — goes in
# ~/.pgpass of the user who runs these commands and owns the crontab, which
# libpq reads by itself, so it appears in no argument and no environment:
#
#   (umask 077 && touch ~/.pgpass) && chmod 600 ~/.pgpass   # keeps any existing lines
#   then, IN AN EDITOR (not echo/printf: shell history keeps what you type), add one line:
#   127.0.0.1:5432:*:postgres:<POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD>
#
# The database field is `*` because restore-drill creates a NEW database each
# run (restore_drill_<time>_<pid>) and .pgpass has no prefix wildcards. Host,
# port and user stay exact, and a superuser can already open any database on
# its cluster, so `*` grants nothing it did not have. libpq IGNORES a .pgpass
# that group or others can read; host-run.sh refuses it first, and says why.
# Never print the file, never copy it into a log, an issue or a backup.
DATABASE_SUPERUSER_URL=postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/engineering_marketplace

# migrator — seed:catalog, seed:payment-methods, bootstrap:owner. The same
# password as DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL in .env.production (set with \password).
# It may keep its password: only Node reads it, inside its own process, and
# never hands it to another program as an argument.
DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgresql://migrator:<migrator password>@127.0.0.1:5432/engineering_marketplace

# Optional. Where backups are written, relative to the checkout by default.
# BACKUP_DIR=/srv/enginora/backups

# Do NOT put OWNER_PASSWORD here. bootstrap:owner asks for it when it is not
# exported; a password at rest in a file outlives the one moment it is needed.
